## 🏛️ 1. Breaking the Trust Between Microservices
In large-scale platforms, developers use an API Gateway or a Web Application Firewall (WAF) at the front door. Because the front door is heavily secured, developers assume the interior hallways are safe. They write internal microservices (like the billing ledger or the casino game sync engine) to blindly trust whatever data the gateway forwards to them. [2, 3] 

* How you break it: You look for routing asymmetries. You use path pollution (/..;/), header injection (X-Forwarded-Host), or double URL encoding to trick the front gateway into thinking a request is completely safe. The gateway trustfully forwards the packet inside the network, where the backend server interprets the true, malicious path and executes it without any internal authorization checks. [4, 5] 

## ⏱️ 2. Breaking the Trust in Time (Sequential Execution)
Developers trust that time moves linearly in their code. They write a function assuming that Step 1 (Check balance) will fully finish and update the database before Step 2 (Deduct balance and payout) can be executed again by the same user.

* How you break it: You introduce concurrency. By compressing 30 requests into a single HTTP/2 TCP network packet, you force the application's parallel processing threads to execute simultaneously. You break their trust in sequential time by forcing five different threads to run Step 1 at the exact same microsecond, before any of them can complete Step 2.

## 🧬 3. Breaking the Trust in Data Models (Mass Assignment)
Developers trust their frameworks. When they use a modern database manager (an ORM), they trust that if the front-end user interface only shows fields for first_name and phone, the backend database will only update those specific fields. [6] 

* How you break it: You exploit hidden object boundaries. You look at the raw network requests and inject hidden administrative keys directly into nested JSON sub-objects ("is_admin": true or "rollover_requirement": 0). If the developer blindly trusted the ORM's automated save functions without writing an explicit field whitelist, the database trustfully processes and saves your injected properties.

## 🎮 4. Breaking the Trust in Third-Party "Glue"
1win does not build its own payment gateways or casino slot machines; they write code to connect their platform to regional third-party APIs. Developers trust that these external providers will always send flawlessly verified, securely signed cryptographic tokens during deposits and withdrawals.

* How you break it: You target the validation handshake. You analyze how the 1win backend processes the provider's callback notifications. You manipulate parameter data types (turning string hashes into boolean arrays or injecting trailing null bytes) to trigger loose type-comparison flaws (== instead of ===). You trick the backend verification engine into trusting a spoofed transaction status without verifying a real bank settlement. [7] 

